# <FILE>docs/design/tui-vfx-v3-schema-draft.json</FILE> - <DESC>Draft V3 recipe schema — specification-by-example with # comment annotations. Stripping lines starting with `#` yields valid JSON representing a canonical V3 recipe.</DESC>
# <VERS>VERSION: 0.8.1</VERS>
# <WCTX>Keep the draft schema annotations aligned with border/title fields, shadow composite modes, and the grid-first V3 snapshot renderer boundary.</WCTX>
# <CLOG>0.8.1: annotate shadow composite modes against the grid-first V3 snapshot renderer seam.</CLOG>

# ============================================================================
# tui-vfx V3 schema — DRAFT
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# This file is a specification-by-example of the draft V3 recipe schema.
# It is the current implementation-target schema baseline after the dual-auditor
# synthesis pass. Future revisions may refine or extend it, but the concrete
# field homes and example wire shape below are the active build contract.
#
# Lines starting with `#` are comments. Stripping them (e.g. `grep -v '^\s*#'`)
# yields a syntactically valid JSON document that serves as the canonical
# example V3 recipe.
#
# This document is the current build-target baseline. Comments may still
# preserve migration-history context and future-extension notes, but the
# concrete field homes and example wire shapes below are now the recommended
# implementation target unless later steering explicitly changes them.
# Companion documents:
#   - docs/design/tui-vfx-v3-upgrade-plan.md           — intent + Decisions 1–8
#   - docs/design/tui-vfx-v3-recipe-vocabulary.md      — canonical vocabulary,
#                                                         rect/scene diagrams,
#                                                         basis language
#   - docs/design/tui-vfx-v3-upgrade-debug-recipes-migration-log.md
#                                                       — schema journal, drift,
#                                                         final V2↔V3 audit
# ============================================================================
# TOP-DOWN AUTHORING MODEL (synthesized after dual audit)
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# V3 now has a recommended authoring posture:
#
#   1. Higher-level family/template authoring (outside this concrete schema)
#      - one template + variants may emit many concrete recipes
#      - keep this above the concrete recipe tree
#
#   2. Concrete recipe envelope (this document)
#      - identity / discovery / contracts at the top level
#      - `config` retained as the authoring envelope for render-relevant data
#
#   3. Renderer trees inside `config`
#      - `content.effect` is a renderer subtree, not just a bag of leaf params
#      - `scene.layers[]` are renderer trees with per-layer sources + pipelines
#
#   4. Pipeline tree
#      - structural nodes: `parallel`, `sequence`
#      - operational leaf kinds: `mask`, `sampler`, `filter`, `shader`,
#        `style_effect`
#
#   5. Policy variants stay inside payloads whenever possible
#      - prefer one deep family with policy axes over many flat sibling names
#      - e.g. split_flap, typewriter+cursor, vignette, edge-grow, sweep families
#
#   6. Hybrid templates and wrapper/router nodes are real concepts
#      - some recipes are best modeled as reusable compositions, not as one leaf
#      - some nodes host another capability family (e.g. style-native spatial)
#
# The practical design target is not just "can the schema encode the corpus?"
# It is "can the schema encode it with an elegant capability tree that stays
# discoverable and maintainable as the library grows?"
# ============================================================================
# STABILITY POLICY FOR BUILDERS
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# - Builders should treat the concrete wire shape in this file as the current
#   implementation target for V3 work.
# - Comments that mention future extensions or alternative ideas are backlog
#   notes, not indications that the core wire shape is still undecided.
# - The normalized IR described elsewhere is a tooling/implementation layer
#   above this author-facing schema, not a prerequisite for writing parsers or
#   runtime models against the concrete fields below.
# ============================================================================

{
  # =========================================================================
  # 1. TOP-LEVEL IDENTITY
  # =========================================================================
  # Every recipe is a self-contained JSON document with a stable identity.
  # These fields are recipe-level metadata and never participate in the
  # pipeline / scope / step semantics below.

  "schema_version": 3,
  # ^ Integer. Always 3 for V3 recipes. V2 used 1. The version is a hard gate:
  #   the V3 loader only loads 3; V2 recipes must be migrated before load.
  #   See V3 plan §"Why now" for the clean-break rationale.

  "id": "example.canonical.v3_recipe",
  # ^ Dotted-identifier string. Unique per recipe; convention is
  #   `<family>.<subfamily>.<variant>`. Used for references + tooling.

  "title": "Canonical V3 Recipe",
  # ^ Human-readable short title. One line.

  "description": "A representative V3 recipe exercising every major schema facet, annotated with per-field semantics.",
  # ^ 1-3 sentences. What the recipe does + any distinctive design choice.

  "version": "3.0.0",
  # ^ SemVer. Bumps on material recipe changes. Convention carried from V2.

  "last_updated": "2026-04-21",
  # ^ ISO date. When the recipe was last reviewed/edited.

  # =========================================================================
  # 2. EXTENDS — recipe inheritance
  # =========================================================================
  # Optional. If present, the named recipe is loaded first, this recipe's
  # config is deep-merged onto it per Intention 39 (tagged-union variant
  # changes replace entire sub-objects; no field-level leak across variants).
  # The wargames/ hierarchy exercises this extensively — a 57-recipe tree
  # extending 10 templates.
  #
  # Paths are repo-relative. Recipe loaders resolve this before applying
  # the rest of the envelope.

  "extends": null,
  # ^ null | string. Example: "themes/new_wopr_fullscreen_cyan.json".
  #   Mutually composable with requires_tokens / requires_bindings / $use
  #   (Open Q #17) though the migration did not exercise multi-mechanism
  #   composition — each recipe used at most one.

  # =========================================================================
  # 3. METADATA BLOCK — discovery + categorization
  # =========================================================================
  # Optional but recommended for the shipped corpus. Not consumed by the
  # rendering pipeline; purely for library-scale navigation, validator
  # heuristics, and the Morris audit (Principle 1).
  #
  # Use `intent_hints`, `expected_visual`, and `visual_tags` for current V3
  # authoring. Migration-era names such as `use_cases` / `aesthetic_tags`
  # remain historical context only; they are not the target vocabulary for new
  # schema examples or authoring docs.

  "metadata": {
    "intent_hints": ["debug_preview", "reference"],
    # ^ Non-authoritative discovery hints for likely fit or authoring context.
    #   Hosts/manifests own routing and binding; this field is not policy.

    "expected_visual": "A compact reference fixture clearly demonstrates the named primitive.",
    # ^ Plain-language visual QA expectation. Strongly recommended for
    #   debug recipes and visual reference fixtures.

    "visual_tags": ["warm", "refined", "composed"],
    # ^ Open-vocabulary visual/motion/family/technique tags. Canonical tokens
    #   seen across the corpus include: warm, cool, soft, premium, theatric,
    #   restrained, minimal, maximal, retro, modern, matrix, noir, crt, glitch,
    #   phosphor, breathing, meditative, urgent, calm. Not a closed enum.

    "mood": "calm",
    # ^ Single-word emotional tone. Candidates: welcoming, urgent,
    #   meditative, energetic, refined, calm, playful.

    "related_themes": ["theme-neutral"],
    # ^ Which gt-design themes this recipe pairs with, or "theme-neutral"
    #   for recipes that aren't theme-bound. Advisory/discovery only.

    "maturity_era": "mature",
    # ^ Which development era this recipe represents — helps the Morris
    #   audit (Workflow B). Values: basic, theatric, mature, professional,
    #   impressively-theatric, experimental.

    "authoring_notes": "Brief intent line or design rationale for readers.",
    # ^ Free-form. Important for recipes that encode non-obvious tuning or
    #   reference specific design judgment (e.g., concealed_light's
    #   source_cutoff+edge_width combo).

    "last_reviewed": "2026-04-21"
    # ^ ISO date. When the recipe was last validated against current design
    #   standards. For the Morris audit.
  },

  # =========================================================================
  # 4. CONTRACT DISCOVERY — tokens, bindings, assets (Open Q #14)
  # =========================================================================
  # Optional. When present, declares what app-provided values this recipe
  # expects. Validators cross-check that every {{token}} reference in the
  # JSON has a requires_tokens entry and that runtime bindings are declared
  # in requires_bindings. Strict-mode loader fails loud if the caller's
  # Substitutions context doesn't satisfy required keys.
  #
  # All three blocks are optional; recipes that need nothing from the app
  # omit them entirely.

  "requires_tokens": {
    # ^ Mustache-style string substitutions into string fields.
    "app_name": { "type": "string", "default": "My App" },
    "brand_color_hex": { "type": "string", "default": "#4080FF" }
  },

  "requires_bindings": {
    # ^ Typed structured-value bindings (ParamValue::RuntimeBinding targets).
    "progress": { "type": "number", "default": 0.0, "range": [0.0, 1.0] },
    "direction": { "type": "enum", "values": ["forward", "reverse", "ping_pong"], "default": "forward" },
    "hovered_row_y": { "type": "u16", "default": 0 }
  },

  "requires_assets": {
    # ^ Asset references (images, fonts) resolved via Substitutions.with_image
    #   / with_font at the tui-vfx-recipes boundary.
    "logo": { "type": "image", "format": "rsb", "canonical_path": "assets/logo.rsb" }
  },

  "requires_primitives": [
    "shader.border_sweep",
    "content.typewriter"
  ],
  # ^ Optional capability declaration carried forward from V2. This is a
  #   library/discovery/tooling hint rather than a render-time control.
  #   Use it when a recipe intentionally depends on specific primitive or
  #   generator families so validator/docs can report capability coverage.
  #
  # V2 pool / preset note:
  # - `text_pool`, `effect_pool`, `preset_pool`, `image_pool`, and `font_pool`
  #   are treated as authoring-scale family/template/content-source concerns,
  #   not as new concrete tree nodes inside one realized recipe. They belong in
  #   the higher-level authoring layer that emits concrete recipes.

  # =========================================================================
  # 5. CONFIG — the recipe body
  # =========================================================================
  # All of the recipe's rendering-relevant data lives under `config`. The
  # distinction from the top-level block is that `config` is what flows into
  # the canonical PlaybackItem builder (Decision 8); everything above is
  # metadata for discovery, inheritance, and validation.

  "config": {

    # -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    # 5.1 Content — the message and optional content effect
    # -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    # `message` is the string rendered into the recipe's cells. Multi-line
    # via \n. Mustache tokens ({{app_name}}) are resolved per requires_tokens
    # before rendering.

    "message": "Welcome, {{app_name}}",

    # `content` (optional) is the content effect — a content-layer factory
    # that controls HOW the message becomes cells (typewriter, scramble,
    # marquee, mirror, morph, dissolve, glitch_shift, split_flap,
    # glyph_cascade, numeric, odometer, redact, slide_shift, wrap_indicator).
    # See Q25 for the home decision: sibling of pipeline for root recipes;
    # per-layer in scene-layer recipes.

    "content": {
      "mode": "enter_only",
      # ^ Tagged-union: enter_only | dwell_only | loop | all. Gates when
      #   the content effect is active relative to the recipe lifecycle.

      "effect": {
        "type": "typewriter",
        "speed_variance": 0.2,
        "cursor": {
          "character": "|",
          "blink_interval": 500,
          "show_while_typing": 1.0,
          "show_after_complete": 1.0
          # ^ Factory-internal nested sub-config. Each content-effect type
          #   owns its own payload shape. Important synthesis result: many
          #   content recipe names are best understood as policy variants
          #   inside deeper renderer trees (e.g. split_flap board-system,
          #   typewriter + nested cursor policy spaces), not as separate
          #   top-level families.
        }
      }
    },

    # -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    # 5.2 Layout, lifecycle, border
    # -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    # Recipe-level geometry and timing envelope.

    "layout": {
      "width": 40,
      "height": 3,
      "exterior_margin": 0,
      "anchor": "center",
      # ^ Locked for the current build-target baseline. `anchor` remains the
      #   concrete field name in V3.
      # ^ `exterior_margin` is the generic host-spacing / breathing-room
      #   control. It is especially useful when a rect host sits near a
      #   viewport edge and needs visible room for attached shadows.

      "mode": "fixed"
      # ^ "fixed" (width/height honored) | "fullscreen" (fill viewport).
      #   For "fullscreen" the width/height fields are ignored.
      #   As-built player contract: fullscreen recipes render against the live
      #   grid supplied by the host render cycle, so a resize-aware app becomes
      #   adaptive simply by passing the current grid dimensions each frame.
      #   Fullscreen effects such as BSOD must own every cell in that grid.
    },

    "lifecycle": {
      "auto_dismiss_ms": 6000
      # ^ Milliseconds after enter completion before exit begins.
      #   0 = no auto-dismiss (caller-controlled).
      #   999999 = effectively never (used for ambient/dwelling recipes).
      #   Locked for the current build-target baseline as `auto_dismiss_ms`.
    },

    "border": {
      "type": "rounded",
      # ^ Tagged-union: none | plain | rounded | double | thick.
      #   `plain` is Ratatui's default single-line drawn border (┌─┐│└─┘).
      #   `single` may be accepted as a compatibility alias for `plain`, but
      #   new recipes and theme tokens should use canonical names.
      "trim": "none",
      # ^ trim policy for border corners. none | vanishing_edge.
      "title": "ALERT",
      # ^ Optional inline border text. This is chrome content, not body copy.
      #   On the native V3 path, title/frame/custom border cells are tagged as
      #   border-role source cells so role-scoped effects can target them. V2
      #   recipes remain on the legacy V2 renderer until migrated; do not infer
      #   V3 role-map parity from a legacy recipe that has not moved to V3.
      "title_position": "top",
      # ^ top | bottom | left | right. Left/right write top-to-bottom on
      #   the vertical border.
      "title_alignment": "center"
      # ^ left | center | right for top/bottom title placement. Left/right
      #   title positions ignore horizontal alignment.
    },

    # -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    # 5.3 Clock — continuous temporal basis (D1 drift)
    # -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    # Optional. V3 renames V2's `time` block to `clock` per Q4. A recipe
    # that declares a clock exposes a looping temporal context that
    # SignalGraph nodes inside the pipeline inherit as their default
    # temporal basis (Q3). Recipes without a clock see signals evaluate
    # against the recipe's own elapsed time.
    #
    # Clock is orthogonal to runtime bindings: clock drives signal
    # evaluation; bindings bypass the clock and are resolved from app state.

    "clock": {
      "loop": true,
      "period_ms": 4200
      # ^ The canonical cycle. Signals inside the pipeline inherit this
      #   period unless they explicitly override via their own period_ms.
      #   Resolved direction: V3 does NOT add a separate Timer primitive at
      #   this stage. Timing remains intentionally distributed across
      #   lifecycle timing, `clock`, `pipeline.timing`, and payload-local
      #   fields that own their own cadence semantics.
    },

    # -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    # 5.4 Base style — recipe-wide default fg/bg (Q1 hybrid resolution)
    # -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    # The fg/bg colors the recipe starts with, before any pipeline step
    # operates. Optional — a recipe with no `base_style` inherits the
    # terminal canvas colors.
    #
    # For single-scope recipes (most of the corpus) this sibling-of-pipeline
    # form is sufficient. For multi-scope recipes where different scopes
    # need different base styles (V2's `styles: [...]` array), authors add
    # `StyleEffect` steps at the head of the pipeline with their scope —
    # see the 5.7 pipeline.step examples below.
    #
    # Modifiers are in-contract for the current build-target baseline. V3 uses
    # one canonical positive list (`modifiers`) instead of V2's
    # added_modifiers / removed_modifiers split.


    "base_style": {
      "foreground": { "type": "rgb", "r": 200, "g": 220, "b": 255 },
      # ^ Color variants: rgb (r/g/b 0-255), named (white/black/red/green/
      #   blue/yellow/magenta/cyan/light_red/light_green/light_blue/
      #   light_yellow/light_cyan/light_magenta), transparent, reset.
      "background": { "type": "rgb", "r": 10, "g": 15, "b": 30 },
      # ^ Q27: V2 occasionally had bare {r,g,b} without the "type" tag;
      #   V3 normalizes to tagged form.
      "modifiers": ["bold"]
      # ^ Canonical V3 shape for style modifiers.
    },

    # -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    # 5.4.1 Regions registry — reusable / compressed selectors
    # -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    # Optional. Large real recipes (status bars, staged banners, scoreboard
    # boards) become unwieldy when they spell every targeted cell literally.
    # V3 now recommends a reusable region registry so the authored pipeline can
    # refer to semantic names instead of giant inline `Cells` arrays.
    #
    # The registry entries are themselves Scope values. This means region
    # definitions can use primitive selectors (rows, row_range, cell_runs,
    # cells, border, content, ...) or compose other region refs indirectly
    # through `and` / `or` at the usage site.

    "regions": {
      "title_band": { "kind": "row_range", "start": 0, "end": 1 },
      "left_status_segment": {
        "kind": "cell_runs",
        "runs": [
          { "y": 0, "x_start": 0, "x_end": 22 }
        ]
      },
      "victory_patch": {
        "kind": "cell_runs",
        "runs": [
          { "y": 1, "x_start": 10, "x_end": 38 },
          { "y": 2, "x_start": 10, "x_end": 38 }
        ]
      }
    },

    "theme": {
      "id": "theme-neutral"
    },
    # ^ Optional. Theme affinity / resolution hint for recipes that are not
    #   fully theme-agnostic. Can be used by catalog tooling and future token
    #   resolution layers without forcing theme semantics into every payload.

    "shadow": {
      "style": "solid",
      "color": { "type": "rgba", "r": 0, "g": 0, "b": 0, "a": 160 },
      "offset": { "x": 2, "y": 1 },
      "inset": { "x": 0, "y": 0 },
      "inset_end": { "x": 0, "y": 0 },
      "falloff": { "x": 0, "y": 0 },
      "edges": ["right", "bottom"],
      "soft_edges": true,
      "source_region": "Border",
      "composite": {
        "mode": "grade_underlying",
        "grade": {
          "fg_dim_strength": 0.40,
          "bg_dim_strength": 0.58,
          "fg_desaturate_strength": 0.30,
          "bg_desaturate_strength": 0.42,
          "fg_tint_strength": 0.10,
          "bg_tint_strength": 0.18,
          "preserve_fg_alpha": true,
          "preserve_bg_alpha": true,
          "replacement_char": "·"
        }
      }
    },
    # ^ Optional. Shadow remains an envelope-level authored surface for the
    #   whole composed recipe. This draft now reflects the real upstream shadow
    #   capability surface rather than a placeholder blur stub.
    #
    #   As built in code, this now maps directly to the typed upstream
    #   `tui_vfx_compositor::types::ShadowSpec` /
    #   `tui_vfx_shadow::ShadowConfig` surface.
    #
    #   Important fields:
    #   - `style` mirrors the shared shadow renderer families
    #   - `color` carries transparent-shadow alpha explicitly
    #   - `source_region` restricts extrusion to role-tagged source cells
    #   - `inset` trims the start of top/bottom and left/right runs; `inset_end`
    #     trims the opposite side so bottom-only shadows can be centered under a
    #     surface, e.g. two cells in from both horizontal edges.
    #   - `falloff` tapers shadow alpha at horizontal/vertical run ends while
    #     preserving transparent/dimmed content under the shadow.
    #   - `side_coverage_eighths` optionally renders left/right solid shadow
    #     columns as foreground block-fraction glyphs over transparent
    #     backgrounds. `6` gives a three-quarter side edge for optical tuning.
    #     Use it with glyph-overlay when you want the visible sub-cell edge;
    #     use blend/grade-underlying when preserving destination glyphs matters.
    #   - `composite.mode` distinguishes glyph-overlay from grade-underlying
    #
    #   These are the typed shadow fields after load-time substitution. Caller
    #   tokenization lives at the `tui-vfx-recipes` boundary; the schema itself
    #   describes the resolved form.
    #
    #   Runtime access note:
    #   V3 snapshots compose through `render_v3_snapshot_onto_grid` or
    #   `render_v3_snapshot_onto_scene`; ratatui's `render_v3_frame_to_buffer`
    #   is only an adapter over that grid-first seam. Shadow semantics do not
    #   belong to ratatui. The grid renderer dispatches `GlyphOverlay` through
    #   the compositor's shared shadow blend helper, `GradeUnderlying`
    #   through the destination-preserving grade helper, and
    #   `BlendUnderlying` through the destination-preserving alpha blend helper.
    #   Use the scene variant when downstream code needs `RoleTag::Shadow`
    #   write-back.

    # -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    # 5.5 Scene layers (Decision 5) — multi-source composition
    # -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    # Optional. Present only when the recipe composes multiple content
    # sources (backdrop + image + text stack + procedural + ...).
    # Canonical example: madeira-flag recipe.
    #
    # When `scene.layers` is present, `message` + `content` at the recipe
    # level are ignored; each layer provides its own content source. Layers
    # may also carry `placement`, `surface`, and `pipeline` siblings. The
    # top-level `pipeline` applies AFTER per-layer pipelines complete, to
    # the composed cell grid.
    #
    # Resolved direction after audit: procedural generators use a registry key
    # (`schema_ref`) for validator-visible typed params, and sibling-anchored
    # placement is a first-class scene capability rather than a side note.

    "scene": null,
    # ^ The canonical stripped JSON keeps `scene` null so the root-level
    #   `message` + `content` remain semantically active in this one concrete
    #   example. Scene layers are still part of the schema; a commented example
    #   shape follows immediately below.
    #
    # Example scene-layer shape:
    # "scene": {
    #   "layers": [
    #     {
    #       "id": "backdrop",
    #       "role_tag": "background",
    #       "source": {
    #         "type": "procedural",
    #         "source_id": "solid_color_fade",
    #         "schema_ref": "procedural.solid_color_fade.v1",
    #         "params": { "target_color": { "type": "rgb", "r": 5, "g": 5, "b": 15 } }
    #       },
    #       "placement": {
    #         "anchor": "fill",
    #         "z": 0
    #       }
    #     },
    #     {
    #       "id": "text_stack",
    #       "role_tag": "content",
    #       "source": { "type": "card", "title": "{{app_name}}", "body": "Line one\nLine two" },
    #       "placement": {
    #         "anchor": "below_sibling",
    #         "sibling_id": "backdrop",
    #         "offset_rows": 2,
    #         "z": 2,
    #         "motion": {
    #           "enter": {
    #             "duration_ms": 700,
    #             "easing": "quad_out",
    #             "route": { "type": "arc", "bulge": -0.2 },
    #             "dynamics": [
    #               { "type": "pendulum", "amplitude": 8, "oscillations": 2, "damping": 2.0 }
    #             ],
    #             "from": { "type": "offscreen", "direction": "from_top" },
    #             "to": { "type": "anchor", "anchor": "center" },
    #             "relative_to": "backdrop",
    #             "follow": { "mode": "maintain_offset", "lag_ms": 80 },
    #             "phase_offset_ms": 40,
    #             "target_anchor": "top_left",
    #             "edge_crossing": { "edge": "top", "border": "vanish", "shadow": "fade" }
    #           }
    #         }
    #       },
    #       "surface": {
    #         # As built on the native V3 scene path, `surface.base_style` and
    #         # `surface.shadow` now flow through scene-layer runtime execution
    #         # rather than being ignored as dead authoring payload.
    #         "base_style": {
    #           "foreground": { "type": "rgb", "r": 240, "g": 248, "b": 255 },
    #           "background": { "type": "transparent" }
    #         },
    #         "shadow": {
    #           "style": "solid",
    #           "color": { "type": "rgba", "r": 0, "g": 0, "b": 0, "a": 150 },
    #           "offset": { "x": 2, "y": 1 },
    #           "edges": ["right", "bottom"],
    #           "soft_edges": true,
    #           "source_region": "Border"
    #         }
    #       }
    #     }
    #   ]
    # },

    # -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    # 5.6 Motion + pipeline — recipe envelope motion plus per-cell operation tree
    # -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    # The recipe now carries two distinct concerns:
    # - `motion` owns recipe-envelope geometry over time
    # - `pipeline` owns per-cell operations and per-step clocks
    #
    # This keeps whole-object motion out of the effect-step tree while still
    # allowing scene layers to carry their own local motion under placement.

    "motion": {
      "enter": {
        "duration_ms": 500,
        "easing": "quad_out",
        "route": { "type": "linear" },
        "dynamics": [],
        "from": { "type": "offscreen", "margin_cells": 0, "direction": "from_top" },
        "via": null,
        "to": { "type": "anchor", "anchor": "bottom_right" },
        "relative_to": null,
        "follow": null,
        "phase_offset_ms": null,
        "target_anchor": null,
        "edge_crossing": { "edge": "top", "border": "vanish", "shadow": "fade" },
        "snap": { "type": "round" },
        "quantize_steps": null
      },
      "exit": {
        "duration_ms": 400,
        "easing": "quad_in",
        "route": { "type": "linear" },
        "dynamics": [],
        "from": { "type": "anchor", "anchor": "bottom_right" },
        "via": null,
        "to": { "type": "offscreen", "margin_cells": 0, "direction": "from_top" },
        "relative_to": null,
        "follow": null,
        "phase_offset_ms": null,
        "target_anchor": null,
        "edge_crossing": { "edge": "top", "border": "vanish", "shadow": "fade" },
        "snap": { "type": "round" },
        "quantize_steps": null
      }
      # ^ `route` is the carrier path through cell space.
      # ^ `dynamics` layers motion treatment over that route (spring, bounce,
      #   pendulum, friction, projectile, orbit, attractor, etc.).
      # ^ `from` / `via` / `to` use the `PlacementSpec` family.
      # ^ `layout.exterior_margin` remains the generic host-spacing / gutter
      #   control when a host needs breathing room near the viewport edge
      #   (for example to visibly accommodate attached shadows).
      #   This is also where the legacy-equivalent extra positioning capability
      #   lives in V3:
      #   - separate entry anchor  -> `enter.from = {type: anchor, ...}`
      #   - separate exit anchor   -> `exit.to   = {type: anchor, ...}`
      #   - custom entry position  -> `enter.from = PlacementSpec`
      #   - custom exit position   -> `exit.to   = PlacementSpec`
      #
      #   These fields are the generic typed hooks for caller-supplied dynamic
      #   positioning too. The API boundary may populate them through
      #   substitutions/bindings, but the schema itself remains typed and
      #   toast-agnostic.
      # ^ `relative_to` / `follow` / `phase_offset_ms` / `target_anchor`
      #   are the first relational-choreography fields for scene-aware motion.
      # ^ `edge_crossing` carries the viewport edge involved plus the border/
      #   shadow behavior to apply there while the moving host is clipped.
      #
      # These are the typed motion fields after load-time substitution.
      # Callers may populate them via `Substitutions` at the API boundary, but
      # the author-facing schema and normalized runtime model operate on the
      # resolved typed form.
    },

    "pipeline": {

      "timing": {
        "enter_ms": 120,
        "enter_offset_ms": 0,
        "exit_ms": 200,
        "exit_offset_ms": 0,
        "enter_ease": "linear",
        "exit_ease": "linear"
      },
      # ^ Optional typed root playback metadata for phase-scoped pipeline
      #   steps. `enter_ms` / `exit_ms` are whole-recipe phase durations in
      #   milliseconds. `enter_ease` / `exit_ease` preserve authoring and
      #   migration intent for the phase envelope; individual leaves may still
      #   carry their own effect-local easing fields.
      # ^ `enter_offset_ms` / `exit_offset_ms` are optional stagger offsets,
      #   especially for scene-layer pipelines. They let a fullscreen recipe
      #   keep the canvas present while child layers begin or release their
      #   phase-scoped effects at different times.
      #   Example:
      #     Layer A timing: { "enter_offset_ms": 0,   "enter_ms": 500 }
      #     Layer B timing: { "enter_offset_ms": 300, "enter_ms": 500 }
      #   Layer B starts 300ms after Layer A because both offsets are relative
      #   to the parent/root timeline. The field is not a named dependency on
      #   another layer; it is an absolute timeline offset for this pipeline.
      # ^ Fullscreen / whole-canvas use case: these timings provide alternate
      #   enter and exit phases even when there is no visible host enter/exit
      #   animation. A fullscreen effect usually already owns the entire grid,
      #   so it may only need a very short enter-only sampler/filter/shader
      #   phase (for example CRT jitter) and a short exit-only fade/release.
      # ^ Runtime precedence: `config.motion.enter.duration_ms` and
      #   `config.motion.exit.duration_ms` take precedence when present because
      #   they describe geometric host motion. Otherwise the direct V3 player
      #   uses these pipeline timing durations before falling back to runtime
      #   defaults.
      # ^ Example: BSOD V3 uses `enter_ms: 120` so its CRT jitter is a very
      #   short, slight enter-phase disturbance rather than a one-second effect.

      # -------------------------------------------------------------------
      # 5.6.2 Step — the per-cell operation tree
      # -------------------------------------------------------------------
      # Optional (Q22). Absent for effect-free recipes (e.g. baseline.json).
      # Present as either a single Step, a Parallel container, or a
      # Sequence container (Decision 3).
      #
      # The tree shape:
      #   Pipeline  ::= Step | { "kind": "parallel", "children": [Step...] }
      #                        | { "kind": "sequence", "children": [Step...] }
      #   Step      ::= { "kind": StepKind, "scope"?: Scope, "phase"?: Phase,
#                    "clock"?: Clock, "interaction"?: InteractionMeta,
#                    "payload": Payload }
      #   StepKind  ::= "mask" | "sampler" | "filter" | "style_effect" | "shader"
      #                | "parallel" | "sequence"
      #   Phase     ::= "enter" | "dwell" | "exit" | "all"  (default "all")
      #   Scope     — see 5.7 below (default { "kind": "all" })
      #
      # Scope and phase propagate down through container children unless a
      # child overrides explicitly. Parallel children all run concurrently;
      # Sequence children run in declared order.
      #
      # Optional per-step clock override when one leaf needs a different
      # temporal basis than the recipe/default layer clock:
      #   "clock": { "loop": true, "period_ms": 1200 }
      # This is the preferred V3 home for V2 style-layer `clock` overrides.
      #
      # Optional Step sibling for interactive recipes:
      #   "interaction": {
      #     "element_id": "bar_yes",
      #     "state_composition": "layered",
      #     "transition_ms": 150,
      #     "easing": "quad_out",
      #     "states": [
      #       { "state": "hover", "geometry": { "character": "█" } }
      #     ],
      #     "accessibility": {
      #       "focus_visible_required": false,
      #       "min_contrast_ratio": 3.0,
      #       "reduce_motion_compliant": true
      #     }
      #   }
      # This is the resolved V3 home for V2 `interaction_states` +
      # `interaction_config`.

      "step": {
        "kind": "parallel",
        "children": [
          # ---- Example 1: a named-composition shader at dwell --------
          {
            "kind": "shader",
            "scope": { "kind": "channel", "value": "background" },
            "phase": "all",
            "payload": {
              # Named-composition factories preserve V2 payload shape
              # verbatim (Decision 2: "V3 ships with primitive-only
              # authoring as the default surface. Named compositions exist
              # as a second surface but start empty and accrue entries
              # only when a specific composition encodes design judgment
              # worth preserving as a named contract.")
              "type": "concealed_light",
              "color": { "type": "rgb", "r": 244, "g": 226, "b": 196 },
              "source": "top",
              "spread": 4,
              "edge_width": 1,
              "source_cutoff": 0.2,
              "intensity": 0.32
            }
          },

          # ---- Example 2: primitive-form shader (Decision 2) ---------
          {
            "kind": "shader",
            "scope": { "kind": "channel", "value": "background" },
            "payload": {
              "type": "colored_overlay",
              # ^ Canonical V3 primitive. ColoredOverlay { color, pattern,
              #   intensity } where Pattern is an open enum of spatial
              #   distributions. See Q8 for Pattern catalog.
              "pattern": {
                "kind": "radial_from_corner",
                # ^ Pattern variants surfaced in the migration (proposed):
                #   radial_from_corner { source, radius, softness, edge_firmness }
                #   perimeter_halo { radius, falloff }          (Q8)
                #   edge_shadow { edges: [...], radius, falloff }  (Q5)
                #   — plus others as new shader-to-primitive reductions land.
                "source": "center",
                "radius": 8,
                "softness": 0.7,
                "edge_firmness": 0.2
              },
              "color": { "type": "rgb", "r": 236, "g": 214, "b": 186 },
              "intensity": 0.28
              # ^ intensity is ParamValue<f32> — can be constant, binding,
              #   or signal. See 5.8 below.
            }
          },

          # ---- Example 3: scoped style lane / style patch ------------
          {
            "kind": "style_effect",
            "scope": { "kind": "region_ref", "value": "title_band" },
            "phase": "all",
            "payload": {
              "type": "base_style_override",
              # ^ Resolved style-lane decision from the audit: scoped style
              #   patches stay inside `style_effect` rather than forcing a
              #   second top-level base-style mechanism or a new StepKind.
              "style": {
                "foreground": { "type": "rgb", "r": 240, "g": 248, "b": 255 },
                "background": { "type": "rgb", "r": 18, "g": 24, "b": 40 },
                "modifiers": ["bold"]
              }
            }
          },

          # ---- Example 4: sibling primitive — GradientOverlay (Q6) ----
          {
            "kind": "shader",
            "scope": { "kind": "all" },
            "payload": {
              "type": "gradient_overlay",
              # ^ Sibling primitive to colored_overlay. ColoredOverlay has
              #   a single color; GradientOverlay carries multi-stop colors.
              #   Decision 2's orthogonal dimensions.
              "gradient": {
                "stops": [
                  [0.0, { "type": "rgb", "r": 255, "g": 100, "b": 50 }],
                  [1.0, { "type": "rgb", "r": 50, "g": 100, "b": 255 }]
                ],
                "space": "rgb"
                # ^ space: rgb | hsl (future: oklch if mcu-terminal-color
                #   supports it).
              },
              "angle_deg": 45.0,
              "intensity": 1.0
            }
          },

          # ---- Example 4: filter step with phase + scope ---------------
          {
            "kind": "filter",
            "phase": "enter",
            "scope": { "kind": "all" },
            "payload": {
              "type": "tint",
              "color": { "type": "rgb", "r": 100, "g": 200, "b": 255 },
              "strength": 0.3
            }
          },

          # ---- Example 5: mask step with phase -------------------------
          {
            "kind": "mask",
            "phase": "enter",
            "payload": {
              "type": "wipe",
              "direction": "left_to_right",
              "soft_edge": true
            }
          },

          # ---- Example 6: sampler step (note phase_offset, not phase) --
          {
            "kind": "sampler",
            "phase": "enter",
            "payload": {
              "type": "sine_wave",
              "axis": "y",
              "amplitude": 0.3,
              "frequency": 2.0,
              "speed": 1.0,
              "phase_offset": 0.0
              # ^ Q29: V2 `phase` payload field renamed to `phase_offset`
              #   to avoid collision with step-level `phase`.
            }
          },

          # ---- Example 7: style_effect step (Q1 hybrid) ---------------
          # Multi-scope recipes lift V2's styles[] array to StyleEffect
          # steps at the head of the pipeline. Each StyleEffect establishes
          # fg/bg defaults for its scope before subsequent steps operate.
          {
            "kind": "style_effect",
            "scope": { "kind": "border" },
            "payload": {
              "type": "base_style_override",
              "style": {
                "foreground": { "type": "cyan" },
                "background": { "type": "rgb", "r": 20, "g": 40, "b": 60 }
              }
            }
          },

          # ---- Example 8: style_effect with canvas-aware fade ---------
          {
            "kind": "style_effect",
            "phase": "exit",
            "scope": { "kind": "all" },
            "payload": {
              "type": "fade_out",
              "easing": "quad_in",
              "to": {
                "kind": "color",
                # ^ Tagged-union: { kind: canvas } (default, fade to
                #   terminal canvas color) | { kind: color, color: ... }
                #   (explicit target color).
                "color": { "type": "rgb", "r": 18, "g": 28, "b": 40 }
              }
            }
          },

          # ---- Example 9: nested parallel with shared scope ----------
          # Containers propagate scope to children. This inner parallel
          # inherits `kind: content, value: text` from its parent below.
          {
            "kind": "parallel",
            "scope": { "kind": "content", "value": "text" },
            "children": [
              {
                "kind": "style_effect",
                "payload": {
                  "type": "base_style_override",
                  "style": {
                    "foreground": { "type": "white" },
                    "background": { "type": "reset" }
                  }
                }
              },
              {
                "kind": "shader",
                "payload": {
                  "type": "highlighter",
                  "color": { "type": "rgb", "r": 255, "g": 255, "b": 100 }
                }
              }
            ]
          },

          # ---- Example 10: Decision 7 — step output hints -------------
          # Step that emits a named hint (sampler) + downstream step that
          # binds to that hint by name (shader). The hint namespace is
          # bounded-but-growing: displacement, sampled_color, cell_density,
          # alpha_mask, and others as real use cases surface.
          {
            "kind": "sampler",
            "scope": { "kind": "all" },
            "payload": {
              "type": "spatial_signal",
              "emits_hint": "displacement",
              # ^ The producer declares its hint name. Downstream consumers
              #   bind to it via Decision 7.
              "signal": {
                # Resolved draft stance: wrapper-based SignalGraph with
                # compositional nodes and hint-readable leaves. See 5.9
                # below for the full vocabulary.
                "kind": "sine",
                "spatial_frequency": { "x": 8.0, "y": 0.0 },
                "temporal_frequency_hz": 0.382,
                "amplitude": 0.15,
                "offset": 0.0
              },
              "axis": "y"
            }
          },
          {
            "kind": "shader",
            "scope": { "kind": "all" },
            "payload": {
              "type": "displacement_shade",
              "binds": {
                "displacement": "displacement"
                # ^ Consumer binds its internal input "displacement" to the
                #   hint also named "displacement". The factory's
                #   deserializer validates that some upstream sibling
                #   actually emits this hint.
              }
            }
          }
        ]
      }
    }
  }

  # ^ end of top-level recipe object
}

# ============================================================================
# 5.7 SCOPE PRIMITIVE — closed algebraic type (Decision 1, Q23)
# ============================================================================
# Scope is uniformly attachable to every Step. Default when omitted:
# { "kind": "all" }. All variants below are canonical as of this draft.
#
#   Scope ::=
#     # --- Recipe-wide + channel ----------------------------------------
#     { "kind": "all" }
#     { "kind": "channel", "value": "foreground" | "background" }
#
#     # --- Area variants (from Decision 1 + Q23 extensions) ------------
#     { "kind": "border" }                                       # Q23: BorderOnly
#     { "kind": "outer", "margins": { "top": N, "right": N, ... } }
#     { "kind": "inner", "margins": { "top": N, "right": N, ... } }
#     { "kind": "rect", "x": X, "y": Y, "w": W, "h": H }
#     { "kind": "rect_exclude", "x": X, "y": Y, "w": W, "h": H }
#
#     # --- Axis-selector variants (Q23 additions) ---------------------
#     { "kind": "cell", "x": X, "y": Y }              # X, Y are u16 OR ParamValue<u16>
#     { "kind": "cells", "cells": [{ "x": X, "y": Y }, ...] }
#     { "kind": "cell_run", "y": Y, "x_start": X0, "x_end": X1 }
#     { "kind": "cell_runs", "runs": [{ "y": Y, "x_start": X0, "x_end": X1 }, ...] }
#     { "kind": "rows", "rows": [0, 1, 7, 8] }
#     { "kind": "row_range", "start": 0, "end": 3 }
#     { "kind": "columns", "columns": [10, 27, 45] }
#     { "kind": "column_range", "start": 0, "end": 18 }
#     { "kind": "region_ref", "value": <named-region-id> }
#
#     # --- Content / glyph predicates ----------------------------------
#     { "kind": "content", "value": "text" | "non_empty" }
#     { "kind": "glyph_matches", "pattern": <regex-string> }
#
#     # --- Semantic-role (theme-resolved at load) ----------------------
#     { "kind": "role", "value": "primary" | "surface" | ... }
#     # As built: role scopes now lower directly into role-aware runtime
#     # regions on the active V3 path (for example border/text/background).
#
#     # --- Escape hatch for novel cases ---------------------------------
#     { "kind": "predicate", "ref": <registered-predicate-name> }
#     # ^ Closed — requires Rust-side registration, not arbitrary eval.
#
#     # --- Algebraic combination ---------------------------------------
#     { "kind": "and", "children": [Scope, Scope, ...] }
#     { "kind": "or",  "children": [Scope, Scope, ...] }
#     { "kind": "not", "child": Scope }
#
# Notes:
# - Cell coords (x/y in `cell` variants, and inside `cells`) accept either
#   raw u16 or ParamValue<u16> (i.e., { "binding": "...", "default": N }).
#   This enables run-time cell targeting — see the style_cell_position
#   _binding debug recipe.
# - `cell_run` / `cell_runs` are the first compression-oriented selectors
#   promoted into the concrete schema. They are aimed at long status bars,
#   big staged banners, and any recipe where contiguous cells are the real
#   authored concept.
# - `region_ref` lets large recipes define readable semantic regions under
#   `config.regions` and reference them throughout the pipeline.
# - Q10: scope alone expresses V2's apply_to vocabulary. `{kind: "channel",
#   value: "background"}` == V2 apply_to=background; `{kind: "all"}` ==
#   apply_to=both. ColoredOverlay tints all channels in scope by default.
# - Q15 (deferred): V2's highlighter.row_mask is scope-encoding inside the
#   factory. Future V3 should lift to step.scope = {kind: "rows"} and
#   remove row_mask from the factory payload.

# ============================================================================
# 5.8 ParamValue<T> — runtime bindings + signals (Decision 6, Q11/Q21)
# ============================================================================
# Any typed parameter on any Step's payload accepts one of three forms.
# This is the V3 generalization of V2's ad-hoc `{field, field_binding}`
# parallel-field pattern.
#
#   ParamValue<T> ::=
#     # --- Constant (the common case) -----------------------------------
#     T                                                  # e.g. 1.5, "forward", { "type": "rgb", "r": 100, "g": 200, "b": 255 }
#
#     # --- RuntimeBinding (app-supplied per-frame) ----------------------
#     { "binding": <name>, "default": T }
#     # ^ `default` preserves V2's offline-validator fallback. Without it
#     #   every binding recipe fails offline validation. Strongly recommended.
#
#     # --- SignalGraph (Decision 6; resolved wrapper shape) -------------
#     { "signal": SignalGraphNode }
#
# Enum-typed bindings (Q12): prefer variant strings (e.g. "forward") over
# V2's u16 ordinals for self-documenting wire format.
#
# ColoredOverlay.intensity being ParamValue<f32> is what lets a primitive-
# form recipe replace V2's per-shader `mode: "breath"` / `mode: "drift"`
# flags with a SignalGraph on intensity — no factory-specific modulation
# flags needed.

# ============================================================================
# 5.9 SignalGraph node vocabulary (resolved draft stance)
# ============================================================================
# V3 consumes mixed-signals (Intention 40). The JSON shape below is the
# concrete wrapper-based draft stance for build-against purposes. If upstream
# mixed-signals eventually prefers a different wire format, canonicalization
# should adapt raw recipes into the normalized IR rather than forcing authors
# to rewrite the conceptual model. The conceptual model itself is now treated
# as settled enough to implement against.
#
#   SignalGraphNode ::=
#     # --- Leaves (canonical mixed-signals primitives) ------------------
#     { "kind": "constant", "value": T }
#     { "kind": "sine",
#       "spatial_frequency": { "x": Fx, "y": Fy },      # optional — 1D signals omit
#       "temporal_frequency_hz": Hz,                    # optional — static signals omit
#       "clock_ref": "config.clock",                    # optional — default inheritance
#       "amplitude": A,
#       "phase_shift": Radians,
#       "offset": Offset }
#     { "kind": "triangle", ... }
#     { "kind": "ramp", ... }
#     { "kind": "keyframes", "points": [[t, v], ...] }
#     { "kind": "noise", "seed": N, "scale": S }
#     { "kind": "spatial_noise", "seed": N, "frequency": { "x": Fx, "y": Fy } }
#
#     # --- Spatial-coordinate leaves (Q31 extension) -------------------
#     { "kind": "sample_norm_x" }        # 0..1 per cell x
#     { "kind": "sample_norm_y" }        # 0..1 per cell y
#     { "kind": "sample_cell_x" }        # raw cell x
#     { "kind": "sample_cell_y" }
#     { "kind": "sample_centered_x" }    # -1..1 centered x
#     { "kind": "sample_centered_y" }    # -1..1 centered y
#     { "kind": "sample_radius" }        # 0..1 normalized radial distance
#     { "kind": "sample_angle" }         # 0..TAU angle around center
#
#     # --- Planned companion surface/frame-space leaves -----------------
#     # These are intentionally distinct from the cell-space leaves above.
#     # They exist because some consumers need continuous frame/surface
#     # geometry rather than edge-inclusive discrete cell-lattice geometry.
#     { "kind": "sample_surface_centered_x" }  # -1..1 centered on frame/surface
#     { "kind": "sample_surface_centered_y" }
#     { "kind": "sample_surface_radius" }      # 0..1 optical/surface radial distance
#     # possible later companion:
#     # { "kind": "sample_surface_angle" }
#
#     # --- Envelopes / physics -----------------------------------------
#     { "kind": "adsr", "attack_ms": A, "decay_ms": D, "sustain": S, "release_ms": R }
#     { "kind": "damped_spring", "stiffness": K, "damping": C, "mass": M, "initial_velocity": V0 }
#
#     # --- Compositional operators (mixed-signals) ---------------------
#     { "kind": "add", "children": [SignalGraphNode, SignalGraphNode, ...] }
#     { "kind": "multiply", "children": [...] }
#     { "kind": "mix", "a": SignalGraphNode, "b": SignalGraphNode, "t": SignalGraphNode }
#     { "kind": "normalize", "child": SignalGraphNode }
#     { "kind": "remap", "child": SignalGraphNode, "from": [a, b], "to": [c, d] }
#     { "kind": "clamp", "child": SignalGraphNode, "min": X, "max": Y }
#
#     # --- Runtime binding inside a graph (permissive composition) -----
#     { "kind": "binding", "name": <name>, "default": T }
#
#     # --- Pipeline hint leaf -------------------------------------------
#     { "kind": "hint", "name": "displacement" }
#
# Resolved draft stance:
# - ParamValue uses the wrapper form `{ "signal": ... }`.
# - Spatial-coordinate leaves are now understood as two explicit families:
#   1. cell-space leaves (`sample_norm_x`, `sample_centered_x`,
#      `sample_radius`, etc.)
#   2. planned surface/frame-space companions for optical falloff consumers.
# - Both families belong in mixed-signals rather than being re-implemented as
#   bespoke per-effect math in shaders/filters/masks.
# - Pipeline step-output hints are readable inside graphs via `kind = hint`.

# 5.10 SUBTREE GUIDANCE — what should collapse vs what should stay distinct
# ============================================================================
# The audit suggests a stable top-down subtree posture:
#
#   Reveal / visibility
#     - reveal geometry
#     - segmented/tiled visibility
#     - procedural breakup / visibility fields
#     - hybrid reveal-field compositions
#
#   Displacement / resampling
#     - wave displacement
#     - fracture / segmented displacement
#     - crt-resampling
#
#   Filter-side families
#     - indicator / progress emphasis
#     - traveling-band / sweep
#     - pattern / procedural texture
#     - motion-treatment filters
#     - field-rendering wrappers
#     - rule-engine families
#
#   Style-side families
#     - transition fades
#     - target-aware fades
#     - color transforms
#     - typography-window effects
#     - dwell modulation
#     - scoped style lanes / style patches
#
#   Content-side renderer trees
#     - split-flap board / renderer system
#     - typewriter + nested cursor renderer subtree
#     - representational formatting / content transforms
#
# The schema intentionally does NOT hardcode these as enums at the step-kind
# level. They are governance guidance for how to organize payload families and
# docs, not a new explosion of top-level leaf kinds.
#
# 5.11 FACTORY-INTERNAL / WRAPPER CONVENTIONS
# ============================================================================
# Some concepts are intentionally carried inside payloads or source-layer
# structures rather than promoted to new StepKinds:
#
# - tagged-union payload discriminators
#   Example: focus_field.shape = rect | ellipse
# - dual-color / multi-color payloads
#   Example: glisten_band.head + tail
# - nested policy spaces inside deep renderers
#   Example: split_flap charset / rolling_flip / from_message
# - wrapper/router nodes
#   Example: style_effect(type=spatial, shader=...)
#   Migration rule: legacy `spatial_shader` on a V2 style block becomes
#   either (a) `style_effect(type=spatial, shader=...)` when it is semantically
#   style-local, or (b) a sibling `shader` step when it should behave as a
#   general pipeline operation.
# - field-rendering wrappers over upstream sources
#   Example: subcell-light interpreting an upstream shader-defined field
# - V2 `styles` arrays / `style` singular both normalize to one tree idiom
#   Example: parallel `style_effect`, `base_style_override`, and `shader` steps
# - cross-lane paired capabilities
#   Example: rigid_shake filter + rigid_shake_style
# - shading families that bind upstream hints
#   Example: displacement_shade / wave_correlated shading over a displacement hint
# - sub-cell / fractional rendering families
#   Example: braille, eighth-block, partial-stripe, and similar render modes
#   stay as payload/render-mode families inside existing leaf kinds unless a
#   future interoperability need forces wider promotion.
#
# Promotion rule: only elevate a concept to the schema surface when keeping it
# payload-internal materially harms interoperability, discovery, validation, or
# authoring ergonomics.
#
# Authoring-scale note (outside concrete recipe shape):
# - template + variants authoring remains important, but it sits ABOVE the
#   concrete recipe tree rather than inside one recipe's StepKind algebra.
#
# 5.12 AUTHORING-SCALE INFRASTRUCTURE (resolved direction)
# ============================================================================
# Two cross-cutting decisions from the dual-auditor synthesis are now the
# recommended direction even though they sit partly outside the concrete JSON:
#
#  A. Canonical normalized IR
#     - Validator, viewer, migration-equivalence tests, and future tooling
#       should operate on a canonical normalized IR rather than raw authoring
#       syntax. This is a tooling architecture decision, not a blocker on the
#       concrete schema wire shape.
#     - Reason: several concepts intentionally have multiple ergonomic
#       authoring shapes (region refs, wrapper nodes, hybrid templates,
#       named-factory vs primitive-form, style-native spatial wrappers).
#     - The raw recipe remains the authoring surface; normalized IR is the
#       tool-facing surface.
#
#  B. Template + variants authoring sits ABOVE the concrete recipe tree
#     - Family/template authoring (e.g. easing-family style expansion) is a
#       separate layer that emits concrete V3 recipes.
#     - This is also the recommended home for V2 pool/preset mechanisms
#       (`text_pool`, `effect_pool`, `preset_pool`, `image_pool`, `font_pool`).
#     - This concrete schema remains for a single realized recipe; it does not
#       absorb template+variants into the StepKind algebra.
#
# 5.13 RULE-ENGINE / GOVERNANCE NOTE
# ============================================================================
# Some payload families are better treated as rule engines or governed
# sub-catalogs than as primitive-vs-composition debates.
#
# Example: `glyph_style`
# - large rule tables
# - repeated across phases
# - rule ordering matters
# - behaves more like a mini rules engine than a small effect leaf
#
# The concrete schema still hosts these as payloads on the existing step kind
# (`filter` in this case), but library governance/docs should recognize them as
# a distinct category.
#
# Resolved architecture notes from this synthesis batch:
# - recipe-envelope motion lives on `config.motion`
# - layer-local motion lives on `scene.layers[*].placement.motion`
# - procedural generators use `source_id` + `schema_ref` + typed params
# - sibling-anchored scene placement is a first-class scene capability
# - canonical normalized IR is the recommended tooling-facing representation
# - template + variants authoring remains above the concrete recipe tree
#
# ============================================================================
# 6. EXTENSION BACKLOG / NON-BLOCKING FOLLOW-ONS
# ============================================================================
# These are cases the migration surfaced as V3-unfixable-without-plan-work.
# Each has a reference to the final audit section of the migration log.
#
# Remaining follow-on extensions after the current synthesis pass:
#
#   A. Celebratory ballistic particles / fireworks still need real implementation.
#      - the schema now models the family as a procedural generator subtree
#      - what remains open is runtime/validator/tooling support, not the conceptual home
#
#   B. Region compression / derived selectors may need richer follow-on forms.
#      - `cell_run` / `cell_runs` + `region_ref` are the first resolved layer
#      - future large corpora may still motivate glyph-patch / segment abstractions
#
#   C. Canonical normalized IR implementation remains a tooling task.
#      - the schema decision is resolved
#      - the implementation still needs to be built
#
# DROP-RECOMMENDED:
#   - pipeline.animation_type (V2 vestigial; no observed behavior).

